# Tree trunk (#349, D80): the decor kind `trunk`, native 28x288 (D122; was 16x288), one frame, standing on the floor; branches are
# one-way Platform / FallingPlatform objects drawn over it. Decor tier (D76): no ink, contour in its own hue, no accent hue.
# Not solid, never moves.
# Bark redraw (#236 art review, 2026-11-21): the flat grey pole read as a lamp post. Now a night trunk in cool slate: a
# darker shadow side on the left (contour f), bark plates split by wandering furrows (f) with lit ridges (r, l) towards
# the moon, which is up and to the right (D92, D114), a broken moon rim (h) on the right edge, a knot (k), a snapped stub
# on the shadow side, and roots flaring to the full 16 px at the floor with a little moss (g) on the shadow root.
# The mean lightness stays at the old pole's (OKLab L about 0.23) so it stays quiet on forest-1's near-black play band
# and forest-0's night blue alike (check_contrast.py, decor tier); values in docs/ART_DIRECTION.md.
# Widened (D122, 2026-11-21, the author: "all branches are not attached to the trunk"): the body is 23 px (frame columns
# 3 to 25, drawn from the object's x - 6, so x - 3 to x + 19), so a branch whose box starts at the trunk's x + 16 (or ends at
# its x - 1) lays 3 to 4 px of its root over the bark. The middle bark columns were copied from the 16 px drawing (each row's
# columns 4-9 and 5-10 repeated after column 7, typed characters only); the top five rows and the snapped crown were retyped.
# The roots now flare to the full 28 px. Drawn behind the play tiles, so a trunk passes behind a ledge (Decor.behindTiles).
size 28 288
# Depth palette (D120, 2026-11-21): the same lightness per key in bark brown (hue 62, C 0.026), moss g, lit edge h a lichen grey.
color f 1a0f06
color d 20150b
color m 251a10
color r 302419
color l 3d3126
color h 454339
color g 1b2416
color k 140a03
frame trunk
